A||N||33||HugGRRRFiero||"Worst Mistakes" and other fun stuff||||10||
Z||000000||HugGRRRFiero||09-11-2000||12:08 PM||||Inspired by the "Wierdest thing you've ever had in your car" thread, I figured I'd start a thread where we could all share our worst mistakes and stories we've had with our Fiero's.<P>Like the stuff we've did while working on our cars and would never tell anyone, for example, like forgetting to put the oil back in after an oil change or for example, one of mine, I'll fess up, I was swaping out a hacked engine harness for a good one.  I had to remove a few spark plugs and noticed nothing was loomed.  After finishing the job at 3:00 am I got some sleep and the next day on my way home from work I felt the engine missing.  Got off at the next exit, now missing very badly.  Pulled into the nearest fast food place, popped the deck and sure enough the spark plug wire got a little too close to exhaust manifold and was on fire! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  I should have known better.  The plugs were very old anyway and I was planning on replacing them.  I've got wire looms from a parts car and new wires so I'm all set now.<P>And if you've got a funny story in general, then feel free to post it here.  For example, I got my Fiero roof panel home in one piece by duck taping it down to the roof of my 85 Regal.  Stayed on even at 50 mph!  I drove it home at night, but I know everyone that seen me in their headlights must have said "What the .... is that?"  <P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||10
Z||000001||ChadMan||09-11-2000||01:02 PM||||I was getting this strange "clunk" from my 88 Coupe when I let out the clutch in 1st gear.  I couldn't tell from inside the car where the noise was coming from, so I had my dad drive the Coupe up and down his street from 0 to 20 mph and back, over and over, with the decklid open and me standing in the trunk with my head down over the engine, listening intently and holding on for dear life.<P>I figured out it was the front transaxle mount, so Dad pulled in the driveway and shut the car off and we went inside to call NAPA.  About five minutes later the cops show up to ask us about "reports we've gotten of you guys doing stunts in the streets with this little sports car."  We just looked at one another and played dumb until they gave up and went away - then laughed our a$$es off!||||reg||10
Z||000002||mshill||09-11-2000||01:17 PM||||Chadman,  You had me laughing my a$$ off.<P>This wasn't my Fiero, but back when I was 17 I restored a 1970 Triumph GT6 and when it was all put back together (about 3am) I decide to start it up.  Well, it cranked right up and it ran for a couple of minutes when I saw a puddle of oil on the garage floor.  In my excitement to get the job done I forgot to hook up the oil pressure gauge tube.  I had to clean up about 4 quarts of oil before I could go to bed.  Thank heavens the tube wasn't on the inside of the car which had new carpet and upholstery.||||reg||1
Z||000003||pollock||09-11-2000||01:21 PM||||I still don't think this was very funny, and i certainly don't tell many people this, but i'll share it anyway.<P>You probably have to know a bit about Ford engines to appreciate this, but here goes.<P>When i first built the 289 for my Mustang, i was young and stupid.  I put roller rockers on it.  OK.  <P>But what i didn't understand was that early small block Ford heads require something called "rail-type" rockers to keep them from pivoting off of the valve stems. The rockers were, in retrospect, clearly wrong, but i brushed it off and continued.  <P>I got the engine running, but couldn't get it to rev up right.  messed around with all kinds of distributor and carb tuning, for a few weeks.  Finally, while trying to figure it out, the engine choked, died, and made some burbling sounds.  Felt like somebody had punched me in the stomach.<P>The first thing i did was loosen the oil drain plug, to see a drop of water come out.  <P>Teardown revealed that about half the rockers had slipped off.  This is why it wouldn't rev.  the valves were probably only opening halfway.  Eventually, one rocker released the keepers, dropping the valve, breaking off the end of it, shattering that piston, and punching a hole in the cylinder wall.<P>Talk about a costly, stupid mistake.  i get in a bad mood just thinking about it, and it's been nearly 5 years.||||reg||9
Z||000004||StuGood||09-11-2000||01:59 PM||||One of the better moves I ever made, was when I was trying to get a Fiero to idle right.  Wanted to see if the IAC was working, so I shut engine off, removed the IAC, plugged its connector back in, and laid it on the plenum.<P>Got in the car, turned ignition on, got out, turned around and started to walk back to the see what the IAC was doing -just in time to see something shoot across my path out of the back of the car.  The plunger, which has a little spring under it, had extended and came completely out (because there was nothing to stop it).  Found the plunger, but the little spring was gone, for the time being.  Got another IAC from salvage yard.<P>Found the little spring several months later, while sweeping the garage.  ||||reg||6
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||09-11-2000||03:23 PM||||I've had my fair share of "I can't believe I did that" so let me just go with the "I can't believe <B>he</B> did that".<P>When I was 19 years old, I just got my first car, a Chrysler 1308GT (remember those?). I took a friend for a ride showing off the car. I stopped at the gas station to get gas. When I returned from paying, I saw he had the hood open and was just closing the oil-cap. When I asked what he was doing he answered "oh, I just filled your water reservoir"...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||80's BOY||09-11-2000||03:58 PM||||last summer while following the chilton's directions on setting the valve lash with my 3.1 on the engine stand i overtightened them too much. when i got to the part where it says "rotate crank" to do the other three pistons i watched in horror as 6 of my 12 pushrods bent all at once. months later after it was all together for the first test drive, i was so excited i only put two lugnuts on the front wheels for a "short" drive. 8 miles from home while doing a u-turn on the highway, the wheel falls off and jams under the sway bar. sheesh!!||||reg||6
Z||000007||ThatRickGuy||09-11-2000||04:21 PM||||Not exactly crazy on my part... but I should have been more watchfull.<P>Back in high school I went from beater to beater, I figure $500 for a car that I can keep running for 1/2 a year or more was better then my foolish friends making payments every month. Well, one week, I pulled the old rust bucket 3.8 Grand Prix into the shop for class. I was just doing some minor tune up work and I was sitting inside when I heard this little flap flap noise from under the hood. I looked out to see one of the class bullies playing with my carb. I yelled at him and he walked off, a few minutes later I went to start the car up with a budy of mine under the hood checking the timing. well, with all the excess fuel in the carb from the kid playing with it, it back fired and shot a huge fire ball out at my budy under the hood. He was okay, but he had a little bit of synged hair on the side of his head. The bully kid was sent to detention and droped from the class  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> My budy and I laugh about it now, but back then it scared the heck out of us!||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||09-11-2000||05:12 PM||||This isn't Fiero related but... While stationed in North Dakota, I had a buddy who was having trouble starting his van after one of those sub-zero storms. He and another guy (not me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) had pulled the engine cover and air breather to play with the choak. It was still very cold so they had the doors closed and the windows only cracked for fresh air. <P>After some tinkering, they decided to try some starting fluid. Well, you guessed it... the motor backfired and the next thing they saw was a big orange flash.<P>When they could focus again, the guy in the passenger seat started laghing at my buddy because he had no eyebrows. But my buddy was laughing too hard to care... because the little furry ball on the top of other guys stocking cap was burning like a candle! It burned a nice little bald spot on top of his head.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000009||mrfiero||09-11-2000||06:07 PM||||I don't know where to start!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>One time I cranked the engine of my '88 GT over by hand to set the timing (I wanted the timing marks to be perfectly aligned).  After fiddling with things and setting the distributor in I started it up.  I heard a bang and shut it down......I forgot to remove the socket wrench!  It bent it up pretty good.<P>While in high school I owned a '76 Mercury Bobcat (an "upscale" Pinto) and one day I was driving it and stopped to fill up.  It had been running strangely and the temp. gauge was doing weird things.  I decided to check the radiator and went to remove the cap.....I turned it one turn before the cap flew off and showered me with hot antifreeze.  I learned my lesson about that!!<P>This isn't car related, but stupid nonetheless.....I was in a bad mood one day while working at the Fiero Factory (almost 2 years ago).  I was trying to get the cap off of a can of black spray paint, but it wasn't cooperating.  I got a screwdriver to pry it off with and being in the mood I was in I sort of stabbed the cap with the screwdriver.  The problem was I missed the cap and hit the can......it exploded and covered my entire face with black paint.  What a mess!  Luckily I wasn't hurt (other than my pride).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I have others, but maybe I shouldn't embarrass myself anymore!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">      <BR>      ||||reg||10
Z||000010||Ralph||09-11-2000||06:27 PM||||This one REALLY cracked me up... I laughed so hard I cried... Still having a hard time trying to type this message. GOOD ONE !<P> <P>"After some tinkering, they decided to try some starting fluid. Well, you guessed it... the motor backfired and the next thing they saw was a big orange flash.<P>When they could focus again, the guy in the passenger seat started laghing at my buddy because he had no eyebrows. But my buddy was laughing too hard to care... because the little furry ball on the top of other guys stocking cap was burning like a candle! It burned a nice little bald spot on top of his head."<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg"> <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000011||contra||09-11-2000||06:27 PM||||heh<BR>dumbest mistake i have made with my fiero is<BR>when i was converting my 85 SE to an 85 GT<BR>i was screwing on the bumper pads and impact pads, and there is a bolt you have to screw in from the inside of the metal bumper. I used a pole from a plunger to apply pressure on the head of the bolt, while i screwed on the nut from the reverse. (as you can see i was really baffled as to how i was supposed to do it properly and i didn't fancy taking off the metal bumper too) So i got it all tightened, and ready to put on the bumper, i got everything on, looked great, and i was dripping with sweat from the june sun. Then i cleaned up my tools, and noticed something wrong. Where was the plunger pole??? I had actually left it inside my bumper!! aargh!! so i had to dissassemble everything and get it out and then put it all back together. talk about being pissed off afterwards. but when it was done, i just sat inside and dozed off.<P>well that's my fierostupidity <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||09-11-2000||07:36 PM||||Not Fiero related but...I've been divorced twice.  'Nuff said.  (BTW--I've got a keeper this time. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000013||2xsess||09-11-2000||08:01 PM||||Well I guess I'd have to say that the stupidest thing Fiero related I've ever done is sell any of mine. <P>But I have done some really silly stuff.<P>One day I was doing a quick tune-up on my 86Gt,(plugs,wires,cap,and rotor)I finally got done hugging the plenum,trying to get the back plugs. I was feeling pretty proud, my father said I wouldn't be able to get to all of them. I put the new wires on the new cap and put it in place. I hopped in to see how much better it ran,I turned the switch and it just cranked,no start. I jumped out of the car (few choice words), I checked all the wires,made sure that I had hooked everything back up, fiddled with everything alittle then tried again still nothing. Finally I looked down at all the parts in the trunk and realized that there were TWO rotors lying there. i pulled the cap and put on the rotor and the car started right up.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Greg Piet||09-11-2000||08:11 PM||||mistaking the cold start sensor for the coolant temp sensor and re-wiring it up that way!<BR>That took a few days to figure out why the car was flooding!||||reg||1
Z||000015||hugh||09-11-2000||08:22 PM||||This is not Fiero related,as I have only had one for about a few months.In my 45 or so years  of working on cars I've done some major dumb stuff.When I was 17 I had a 1956 Chevy convertable.It needed a clutch so I put it in,the only problem was I cleaned all the grease off the throwout bearing with gas (made it nice and clean). I drove the car to Atco raceway I used the clutch till it started to squeal,I drove home without using it unless it was absolutely necessary.It seems the dry bearing that was so clean had seized and burned the end of the throwout bearing arm off.One more thing,As I was under the car,I sneezed,being about 17 or 18 my reactions were very good,I almost killed myself under that damn car by the time my head stopped bouncing back and forth from one part of the frame to the other. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||rockcrawl||09-11-2000||08:56 PM||||OK, here we go. <P>'79 F150, I wore the brake rotor down to the fins. Same truck, had a wheel/tire (35" mudders) fall off because I didn't tighten the lugs- broke a windshield on the car behind me. Same truck, backed OVER a BMW the day before I traded it in, no damage to truck. <P>Countach replica, drove it a week after it got painted, without the big scoops bolted down, they landed in the grass. Ran out of gas and had to walk, at least twice- Duh!!. Turned around on a cloverleaf because I realized I was running out of gas- busted up the front air damn and lights- a week before the new owner took delivery. Drove it from PA to Maryland to deliver to new owner- ran out of gas on the freeway!<P>Drove a motorcycle into the woods and crashed into a tree.<P>Drove my 69 Camaro off the road and into a dirt pile.<P>Drove my Kawasaki Ninja into a Deer.<P>Fieros-Did the IAC valve thing and shot the plunger across the room. ran out of gas at least three times.<P>Work van- ran out of gas once.<P>86 F150 ran out of gas once.<P>Rolled my 58 Willys twice, but that was fun. Pumped a tank full of diesel into it, then remembered it had a gasoline engine! Luckily it was already being towed by my truck.<P>Most of this stuff happened when I was in high school, except for the running out of gas thing. I seem to have a problem with that. Need I go on? <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 09-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Gokart||09-11-2000||09:28 PM||||Thank God I haven't pulled any of my "oncores" yet on my Fiero, but I did have a Honda hatchback that wouldn't start one winter morning and I sprayed so much starting fluid in the carb that it caught fire <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> Then I tried to put out the fire with my car seat covers! The covers lit up and melted all over the engine <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> That was one way to kill a ricer <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Gokart_sig_1.gif"> <P>Marc                     87GT||||reg||1
Z||000018||Philphine||09-12-2000||02:26 AM||||i have a footwell mounted sub in my'87 fiero. to make it more secure i drilled holes in the footwell wall and console. if you reach in at the end of the console and feel at the top you'll feel 2 layers of metal. the two layers only go down so far. at the bottom if you could feel that far there is only one, but i didn't know that so when i drilled through it and felt the bit hit metal i kept drilling. next thing i know gas is coming in around the drillbit. i realized what i had done and my second thought was " i could have blew myself up just then". but i can say now that i know how to take out the gas tank.  ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Standard||09-12-2000||05:24 AM||||Hmmm.. I'm still waiting for my glorious moment of stupidity  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  The worst things I've done so far are run out of gas twice (once in each car) and sheared off the heads of the sway bar mounting bolts on the drivers side of my 84.. still haven't gotten up the motivation to try and get those freakin' things out of there...<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000020||AkursedX||09-12-2000||09:55 AM||||Well my most embarrasing Fiero story would be running out of gas on my 1st date with my girlfirend, but at least she didn't dump me!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As for the worst thing that's hapened to me while working on car was with my '88 Diplomat.  My dad used to buy old police cars and fix them up and sell them.  Well he bought me an '88 Dodge Diplomat (damn good car) when I was 16.  While we were dropping the new engine in, I was in charge of the hoist.  Well the engine was up in the air and I was slowly lowering it in while I was moving it foward.  Well it got to the point where my dad told me to tighten stop the hoist.  Well instead of tightening up the screw-thing for the hydraulics, I loosened it, and WHAM!!!  Dropped the engine right on the front end of the car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I was in such shock that I did it, I didn't even jack it up again.  Fortunately, all it took was some minor cosmetic repair to the front end, and the car was good to go.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000021||TheFiero64||09-12-2000||05:26 PM||||The worst things I've done on a Fiero was replacing the missing Heat riser tube on Jaygee79's 84 SE. I noticed the tube was missing so I got one and put it on. I was later pulling out of  the Mc Donald's drive through when I looked back and saw flames from the 84 center vents. I hit the trunk pop and dowsed the flames with the large soft drink I had just bought. Come to find out the tube was part of the recall and the wires were too close to that area. It had minimal damage to the wire harness thanks to my quick reaction. I did however crack the manifold into 3 pieces. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> What a scary thing that was to see in the rear view<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000022||WKDFIRO||09-13-2000||06:19 AM||||I had just finished putting a rebuilt engine into my wife's 87 4 cyl Automatic. I pulled it out of the garage and to my absolute horror I see new oil on the ground. I crawl under and see the oil dripping from between the engine and tranny. <BR>    After a flurry of cursing and ranting about how I could have forgotten an oil plug or not put enough gasket on the cam cover I went in the next day and took it all back apart. As Im looking around the completely clean, back side of the engine I notice the oil dripping again. I traced it back to a bolt holding the coilpacks that actually goes through an oil galley. Its a bolt thats supposed to have some teflon on it otherwise, guess what it leaks oil! Worse than that the oil drips down and went down one of the ribs of the block and drips (when the angle is right)right at the engine and tranny. Spent the rest of the day putting it back together and then 5 minutes to pull the bolt slather some teflon on it and put it back in. No oil drips since.<P>Somebody join me in a DOH!<P>DOH!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 09-13-2000).]||||reg||11
Z||000023||Jaygee79||09-13-2000||08:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TheFiero64:<BR><B>The worst things I've done on a Fiero was replacing the missing Heat riser tube on Jaygee79's 84 SE.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, that was a day I'll never forget  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000024||wkayl||09-13-2000||10:08 AM||||Back in about 1975 I swapped cars with a friend of mine for a few days.  He had an earlu 70's Fiat Spyder.  One day I was driveing it and the engine locked up.  Even pulling the car wouldn't make it turn.  Another friend was a VW owner so he knew a little about pulling an engine out of a rear engine car.  We were going to overhaul it.  After taking everything off the back of the Fiat except the last4 bolts holding the engine to the transaxel, my friend happened to grab the generator and play with the pully.  It was locked. we then grabbed the carnkshaft pully, it turned.  A $2 bearing fixed the car.  The gen belt was so tight that it wouldn' let the engine turn and even slide the tires when pulled.||||reg||1
Z||000025||stimpy||09-13-2000||08:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WKDFIRO:<BR><B>I <BR>Somebody join me in a DOH!<P>DOH!<P>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 09-13-2000).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>doh!  <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page3/homer3d.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||2xsess||09-13-2000||11:52 PM||||Well This one isn't Fiero related but....my father bought an 87 Bronco II that needed an engine, picked it up cheap. The only place to work on it was my grandmothers garage. Dad didn't have an engine hoist, but he did have a come-a-long. Keep in mind that the garage was built in 1974. Dad took 2 large eyehooks and screwed them into the ceiling, thinking that he had put them in the studs. Then he put a pipe through them and attached the come-a-long to it. We were setting the new engine I was holding the engine steady as we lowered it into the engine bay. All of the sudden there was a loud crack and something slammed me on the top of my head.<BR>When I finally stopped seeing stars I looked up to see the ceiling hanging down where the eyehook was. Dad had just barely hit the edge of the stud. The stud had cracked and the pipe bearing the weight of the engine had cracked me on my head. <P>Thats a night I'll never forget.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||2xsess||09-13-2000||11:53 PM||||oops double post  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 09-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||ThatRickGuy||09-14-2000||10:30 AM||||Okay, I just realised I made a new mistake. The engine has been out of the fiero for like 3 monthes now. of course I left everything else in the cradle just for storage reasons. Well, at the shop I use my car gets stored in a fenced in lot. I went out yestarday to work on cleaning up some more parts and start to get it prepped for the new engine. Then I knowticed that the cat must be clogged. now, I wouldn't have had any idea that it was cloged if it wasn't for the standing rain watter all the way up to the top of the cat pipe. So now I need to find a piece of pipe, 2 clamps, and a hack saw.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Terrible Tom||09-27-2000||12:09 AM||||We had our local Pontiac Dealer replace the clutch in our 86 SE a number of years ago (cost about 900 bucks).  After about 60,000 miles on the new clutch, it was time for another replacement but this time I was going to do it myself.  While unbolting the pressure plate, the cluth fell to the floor.  Upon inspection, we saw the springs on the back of the clutch were almost worn down completely.  We immediately looked at the flywheel and saw a lot of wear on the flywheel bolts.  Had we been driving 60,000 miles with a clutch installed backwards?  I can't swear to it cause I didn't see it in backwards (it fell out when taking off the pressure plate), but the evidence looks pretty convincing!||||reg||1
Z||000030||Archie||09-27-2000||07:43 AM||||My first V-8 Fiero was a slightly-built 327 installed into an '84 Se<BR>with the 4-speed.   I had previously built the engine for my '67 Camaro,<BR>but later pulled it out in favor of a Big Block.   This 327 would run<BR>6500 RPM all day long & several times I had peaked it at 7300 RPM, so<BR>suffice to say it was a screamer.<P>I used to be real active in TSD rallies.  For those that don't know,<BR>these are Time, Speed Distance Rallies, these events took place on<BR>public mostly country roads.   The concept of a TSD Rally is that you<BR>should not have the reason to speed or be other wise dangerous to<BR>everybody else who is out driving around on back roads on any given<BR>Sunday afternoon.  (but that's not much fun)  In those days we used to<BR>have these rallies on every old dirt country road the Rally Master could<BR>find, that was back in the days BWO, "Before the rallymasters Wimped<BR>Out", and decided that most of the club members didn't want to get their<BR>cars dusty. (bummer)<P>It was the spring of 1987, a real nice sunny Sunday afternoon, it had<BR>rained a lot the night before and I would say the dirt roads were damp,<BR>tightly packed and very fast. (sounds like a horse race don't it?)  <BR>There were some puddles on the inside of many of the turns.   In our<BR>part of the country the old country roads wander in and out, left and<BR>right.   Many of these roads date back to the old horse and wagon days<BR>and have been cut down into the turf to the point that these old dirt<BR>roads would have what you could call a "curb" that was made of the sod<BR>of the adjoining farmland.<P>I, of course, was driving and my navigator was Larry (last name<BR>withheald upon request).  We were a pretty good team & had won more than<BR>our share of events, usually our regular competitors, upon seeing that<BR>we were at an event, would readily admit that they were competing for<BR>second place.   The Rally started out normally enough and before long we<BR>were on the dirt backroads of Berrien County Michigan.   Larry & I<BR>somehow managed to get lost very badly early on.   (I think it was his<BR>fault)  Upon realizing that we were out there, where ever we were, all<BR>alone we scanned down the instructions for a clue as to where we should<BR>be and where we might pick up the course again.   We found a familiar<BR>location that was about 15 miles Diagonally across the County from where<BR>we were.<P>As I said before, a TSD rally is not meant to be a speed event, however<BR>when you find yourself 15 miles from where you should be, the "Time" in<BR>TSD takes on a whole new meaning.   We set off for our destination and<BR>as we were headed Diagonally across the County, you can assume we were<BR>to be negotiating a number of turns on route.   Now I need to mention<BR>that I was very familliar with the handling of this car and was well<BR>aware what it took to take turns quickly on the dirt.   As you can<BR>imagine we were racing across the county at speeds approaching 100MPH on<BR>tight narrow dirt roads.   Anyone who is familliar with this part of the<BR>country will tell you that on country roads, it is not very often that<BR>you find a road that goes more than 2 miles before abruptly turning<BR>right or left for no apparent reason.   Approaching everyone of these 90<BR>degree turns there is a diamond shaped sign with an arrow pointing the<BR>way and one of those little yellow turn speed signs right under it<BR>("15", "20" or whatever).   I was getting a pretty good routine going.  <BR>I was hitting 100+ on the straights (although I didn't look down much)<BR>and was braking hard down to maybe 50-60 at the start of the turn, I<BR>would then drop into 2nd and nail it thru the turn.   That would 4-wheel<BR>drift me out to the outside "curb" as I accelerated away.  (what a<BR>kick!!!)  Larry, who would normally be quiet during this type of<BR>session, must have been getting a bit apprehensive.   As we approached<BR>the turns he started telling me what direction the arrow was pointed and<BR>what the speed sign said.  But we are still flying.   Larry kept doing<BR>that and each time I'm thinking to myself  J.C. Larry, I can see the<BR>F---ing sign.   On one turn in particular, I remember it well (I could<BR>take you there now), While I'm thinking to myself "J.C. Larry, I can see<BR>the F---ing sign" I forgot to downshift and am very late braking.   OH<BR>OH the turn is starting & I'm still in 4th at about 90....ooooops.   Not<BR>a word said (aloud anyway) I slam 2nd gear yank the wheel to the right<BR>and try to power thru it, I slide out and both left tires hit the<BR>"curb".....flip bounce on the roof and land on the wheels about 50 feet<BR>out in the field.   Engine still running, look at Larry, he's covered in<BR>mud and there is a puddle on his seat, nail the throttle and motor on, a<BR>little slower now, like nothing had happened that wasn't planed.<P>That was our last Rally together, Larry mumbled something about teaching<BR>afternoon Sunday school Bible classes.   Never saw him again, and never<BR>could find a replacement navigator who could keep me winning like Larry<BR>could.<P>Moral of the story......Don't drive like an idiot, you could screw up a<BR>good thing.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000031||mrfixit58||09-27-2000||08:27 AM||||Good story Archie. Maybe a set of curb feelers would have helped.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000032||whamel||09-27-2000||10:37 AM||||My list of 'worst mistakes' is too long to post here.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Cliff would run out of storage space from dumb things I've done over the years alone.<BR>Wade  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000033||Haze_Performance||09-27-2000||11:52 AM||||Worst Mistake "Fiero" :<BR> Not buying a 85 SC from a buddy in 1992. He was a little rich boy. He would have taken $100.00 for it. It was winter and it had a flat tire. It was buried in the snow for 3 months. The problem was that the guy he got it from cut the springs so low you couldnt drive the damn thing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Worst Mistakes "NoN-Fiero" : <BR> 1. Driving a 1973 Buick Centry through an ally in a HUGH snowstorm. I was assing around and hitting snow piles. The last one wasnt a snowpile, It was a large snow covered boulder. OUCH!!<P> 2. After installing a new Intake & Holley 700 double pumper on my 1972 LeSaber, I tried to start it for about 45 mins. Then <B>BAM</B> ........ The mufflers explode. I tore both entire mufflers off the car, cut a brake line & put nice dents in the floor pan.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><BR>This has been a another Have orginal  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2d_00010aa3.gif">  <P><B>Website's<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A><P>                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B>||||reg||9
